## Generic Axis Lengths

Functions can use arrays with a given number of axis with generic axis lengths by using symbolic names for each axis length.

For example: 
```
func add(float32, [_A][_B]float32) [A][B]float32
```
defines an `Add` function which takes two arguments: a `float32` scalar, and an array of `float32` with two axes of generic axis length, named `A` and `B` respectively. `Add` returns an array with the same type (same axes, same data type). 

We use the notation `_A` to assign the axis length of the parameter to the variable `A`. This notation is temporary and will be changed when GX will use its own AST instead of Go AST.


```code:main
package main

func Add(a float32, b [_A][_B]float32) [A][B]float32 {
    return a+b
}

func Main() ([1][2]float32, [2][3]float32) {
    // In this call, A and B in Add will be equal to 1 and 2 respectively.
    a := Add(-10, [1][2]float32{{1, 2}})
    // In this call, A and B in Add will be equal to 2 and 3 respectively.
    b := Add(10, [2][3]float32{{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}})
    return a, b
}
```
